# Pinned package manifest for the MULE image.
#
# STATUS: EMPTY. No package set has been selected, because no userland release
# and no kernel have been selected. See TBR-LINUX-01 and FML-ADR-022.
#
# THE PINNING RULE
#
#   Every package is pinned to an exact version. No version ranges, no
#   "latest", no unpinned transitive dependency. A package that cannot be
#   pinned does not go in the image.
#
# The rule exists because a node in the field is diagnosed by its compatibility
# set version (FML-ADR-040). If two builds carrying the same set version can
# contain different package versions, that identifier means nothing and the
# whole promotion discipline is decorative.
#
# CHANGING A PIN
#
# A change to any pin in this file creates a NEW CANDIDATE SET, which must pass
# the full promotion gate in os/release/README.md: rebuild all out-of-tree
# modules, boot, enumerate every radio, form a mesh, serve the access point,
# pass a traffic smoke test, survive a reboot, and demonstrate rollback.
#
# Dependency update proposals are opened automatically and are NEVER
# auto-merged. Promotion of a dependency is a decision. A green CI run is not
# evidence that the set still works, because CI has no radios.
#
# FORMAT
#
#   <package-name>=<exact-version>    # <why this package is in the image>
#
# One package per line. A comment on every line saying why it is here; an image
# nobody can justify line by line is an image nobody can shrink later.
#
# Radio userspace (iw, wpa_supplicant, hostapd, batctl) is pinned here AND
# recorded in os/kernel/PINS.md, because it is part of the compatibility set.
# The two must agree. Keeping the human-readable set summary in PINS.md and the
# machine-readable pins here is deliberate; a mismatch between them is a defect.

# --- No packages selected. ---
